<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Demetrius of Phalerum</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 350–c. 280 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> · Also known as Demetrius Phalereus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Demetrius of Phalerum was an Athenian statesman and Peripatetic philosopher, a pupil of Theophrastus. Installed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassander">Cassander</a>, he governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> from 317 to 307 BCE, reforming its laws and limiting luxury, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demetrius-i-of-macedon">Demetrius Poliorcetes</a> expelled him. He later went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, where he is said to have advised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-i-soter">Ptolemy I</a> on founding the Library.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/demetrius-of-phalerum.jpg" alt="Demetrius of Phalerum" width="320" height="395" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Yair Haklai · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bust_of_Demetrius_Phalereus_at_Kunsthistorisches_Museum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
